it's great to be joined today by Sarah  Kenzi or a journalist who focuses on  politics the economy and media she's a  regular contributor to The Globe and  Mail you wrote this great article Sarah  in the correspondent called our fate was  sealed long before November 8th relating  of course to the election of Donald  Trump on November 8th let me just first  get a general sense from you what did  you mean by that what suggested to you  that the fate was sealed prior to the  election oh I was talking about  structural problems in the United States  that predate the election and that were  exacerbated by the campaign's during the  election in particular I was talking  about race racism white mob violence you  know conspiracy theories attacks on  freedom of the press the allure of  demagoguery you know all these sort of  things that we've seen emerging during  the Trump campaign when I wrote it  most people thought that that Clinton  was going to win the election I was  always less sure of that  despite the polls because you know I  live in Missouri and I was witnessing  what was happening around me and I saw  these these structural trends in action  and so you know I kind of felt like  either way whether Trump won or whether  Clinton won we were going to be dealing  with potential violence with racist  violence white mob violence hate crimes  and that's indeed what happened after  Trump won the only question in my mind  was whether the sorts of crimes would be  sanctioned by the incoming  administration as I do believe they are  by Trump or whether they would be  opposed as I think they would have been  under Clinton so let's dig into that a  little I mean I'd love to hear what what  your thoughts are in terms of the  sanctioning because on paper you know  Donald Trump gave this insanely tepid  pleasestop type of comment I think  during his first 60 minutes interview  when he was told about the violence that  that was taking place at the same time  we are seeing the selection to Donald  Trump's cabinet and advisors of  individuals with sort of spotty past  with regard to everything from race  relations to understanding sort of the  the real  differences between different  socio-economic classes in the United  States give me a sense of what you're  seeing from Trump that you would sort of  call sanctioning of that type of  violence or discrimination well I could  just you know to go to the points that  you just made Trump has the opportunity  to call out you know racism bigotry  xenophobia anti-semitism all the things  that we've seen happening in a very  forceful way and you know I know  personally if someone were running  around the country screaming my name and  then spray-painting swastikas are  beating up little Muslim children you  know I would be worried about that I'd  be personally offended by that and if I  were in a position of power I would  certainly do all I could to stop that in  order to protect people he doesn't seem  to want to do that protecting American  people it's not a priority for him and  so he's been you know very passive he's  letting it happen I think he knows that  you know it helps to have this kind of  mob response should something go wrong I  don't think that the people doing this  are really representative of most Trump  supporters but I do think that Trump has  an obligation when people are committing  acts of violence in his name to call  them out but then you look at the  cabinet you know you look at the  selection of people like Jeff Sessions  for example who has a long history of  racism or Michael Flynn you know and  generally speaking a cabinet that in  particular is very anti-muslim it's very  Islamophobic and then you look at you  know Trump's own campaign with  endorsements by the KKK and by you know  other white supremacists extremists who  he never really denounces and who have  certainly helped his cause and  contributed to him I don't think he has  any interest in stopping this he's  aligned even with foreign white  supremacists abroad with a growing  movement including people like Nigel  Faraj Vladimir Putin who has hosted  white supremacist in Russia for training  and guidance so I think that this is  something he wants sometimes he's pretty  overt about it most of the time he just  kind of tries to dodge the issue or make  it seem like it it's not a foremost  importance to him but it's certainly  beneficial to him and I don't see him it  had you know honoring that  moral obligation or presidential  obligation to protect vulnerable  citizens who are the victims of these  crimes in a second I want to dig in a  little bit to the anti-semitism aspect  of this but before we do that based on  the the cabinet picks and advisors  you've seen so far I think that there's  sort of an implicit sense when we see  the past statements of many of these  individuals you mentioned Jeff Sessions  you mentioned Flynn etc there's a sort  of sense that this is not going to be  good for marginalized groups but I'm  curious what you would see in terms of  the actual governmental actions that  might be taken by these in these  individuals in terms of policy what  potentially is most concerning to you  and by which cabinet member I mean we  could look at Jeff Sessions and the war  on drugs for example we could look at  Ben Carson and housing policy but what's  most concerning to you I mean everything  is concerning to me and the reason I say  everything is because I think it's  connected I think Trump's goal is maybe  a little bit different than his advisors  but Trump's goal is to build a  kleptocracy that's going to you benefit  his family that's going to strip the  country down for parts and use it to  benefit his his own personal finances  and wealth and that's been going on this  whole time so I think that that's you  know what what's in it for Trump I think  that the cabinet has other objectives in  mind I think that some of this is  extending that you know he's 